{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## OPERATOR-RATIFIED DECISION (2026-08-04)\n\nThe operator ratified the relation-scoped fix, resolving the ticket's 'Options (deliberately not pre-decided)' section in favor of option 1, with a design constraint that keeps the public surface backward compatible:\n\n1. Add a RELATION-SCOPED removal seam at the graph layer — removal keyed on (target_id, relation), symmetric with graph/_links.add_dependency.\n2. Make the reconciler's inbound link-removal path use it, so G5's decline case instead CONVERGES by removing exactly the mirrored relation. The safety invariant is kept: no link the peer still carries is ever removed — a removal naming a relation with no matching net-active local link is a logged no-op.\n3. CLI `unlink` gains an OPTIONAL relation argument (`rebar unlink <id1> <id2> [relation]`); with no relation the existing pair-scoped most-recent behavior is UNCHANGED (backward compatible). The library facade `rebar.unlink` gains the same optional `relation=None` keyword.\n\nThis supersedes the earlier follow-up ruling's open design question ('changes the public rebar.unlink signature or needs a lower-level seam') — the answer is BOTH, backward-compatibly: a lower-level graph seam plus an optional-arg extension that leaves existing call sites untouched."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785860628697764001,"uuid":"11f4d722-9e8d-4fa8-9c87-52f82c206107"}